  </div>

  <div class="pm-pair">
    <figure class="tile pm" style="margin:0">
      <div id="pm-sep"></div>
      <figcaption>separated: positive above the axis, negative mirrored below</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>


  <h2>0c · Reference: the two walks</h2>
  <p class="lead">The same curve drawn from its coordinates, for reading rather
    than calculating: orange positive above the axis, blue negative below, every
    station labelled. It is the picture the builder reproduces.</p>
  <div class="pm-pair"><figure class="tile pm" style="margin:0">
    <Fragment set:html={reference} />
    <figcaption>reference diagram · the stations are written on the drawing</figcaption>
  </figure></div>

  <h2>0d · The Lissajous maker</h2>
  <p class="lead">A new family for this registry: x = T<sub>m</sub>(t), y = T<sub>n</sub>(t),
    the Chebyshev form of the Lissajous figure. Whole coefficients, so every rational
    t gives a rational point — grid 1, not an approximation. Move the two degrees.</p>
  <div class="lab" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:380px minmax(0,1fr);gap:20px;align-items:start">
    <svg id="liss-plot" viewBox="0 0 360 360" role="img" aria-label="the Lissajous curve"
      style="width:380px;height:380px;background:#fffdf7;border:1px solid var(--pico-muted-border-color)"></svg>
    <div>
      <label for="liss-m">x = T<sub>m</sub> · <output id="liss-m-out">3</output></label>
      <input id="liss-m" type="range" min="1" max="12" step="1" value="3">
      <label for="liss-n">y = T<sub>n</sub> · <output id="liss-n-out">5</output></label>
      <input id="liss-n" type="range" min="1" max="12" step="1" value="5">
      <p class="facts" id="liss-readout" style="font:12px/1.6 ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace;color:var(--pico-muted-color);margin-top:12px"></p>
      <p class="lead">Known to the field as Lissajous curves; new to this registry. The
        Chebyshev form is the one that stays on the integer grid.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>1 · Adding curves, term by term</h2>
  <p class="lead">Each term drawn alone over the same window, three across, then the
    sum at the same scale. A polynomial's shape is the sum of its terms; this is
    the only way to see that happen.</p>

  {(data.adders || []).map((adder) => (
    <section>
      <h3 style="font-size:1rem;margin:18px 0 4px">{adder.name}</h3>
      <p class="lead">{adder.note}</p>
      {rowsOf(adder.terms).map((row) => (
        <div class="row">
          {row.parts.map(({ term, plus }) => (
            <Fragment>
              <figure class="tile">
                <Fragment set:html={plot(term.points)} />
                <figcaption>the term {label(term)} alone</figcaption>
              </figure>
              {plus ? <div class="plus">+</div> : null}
            </Fragment>
          ))}
          {row.continues ? <div class="plus">+</div> : null}
        </div>
      ))}
      <div class="row sum">
        <div class="plus">=</div>
        <figure class="tile">
          <Fragment set:html={plot(adder.sum, { size: 560 })} />
          <figcaption>the sum: all {adder.terms.length} terms added</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>2 · The quadrature variable: a quarter turn in place of x</h2>
  <p class="lead">Replacing x by a quarter turn makes the even powers real and the odd powers
    a quadrature — 90° out of phase — so
    the polynomial becomes a plane curve: even terms give x, odd terms give y, and
    every sign rotates. With only odd powers x is identically zero — a straight
    line, kept here rather than hidden.</p>

  <div class="imag">
    {(data.quadrature || []).map((curve) => (
      <figure class="tile">
        {curve.degenerate
          ? <p class="facts">degenerate: x is identically zero</p>
          : <Fragment set:html={plot(curve.points, { size: 340 })} />}
        <figcaption>
          <b>{curve.name}</b><br>{curve.note}
          {curve.degenerate
            ? ""
            : <span class="facts">grid {curve.grid} · inflections {curve.inflections} · bend {curve.bend}</span>}
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>3 · The recipe: a mirrored pair and a stack at ½</h2>
  <p class="lead">Roots at p/q and (q−p)/q, plus a stack at 1/2 — the combination
    the operator remembers. Each term is now a <b>pair</b>: the x term and the y
    term at the same power, drawn as one small arc; the arcs add to the curve.</p>

  {(parametric.rootAdders || []).map((adder) => (
    <section>
      <h3 style="font-size:1rem;margin:18px 0 4px">{adder.name} · {adder.crossings} crossing pairs</h3>
      <p class="lead">{adder.note}</p>
      <div class="row">
        {adder.terms.slice(0, 6).map((term) => (
          <figure class="tile">
            <Fragment set:html={plot(term.points)} />
            <figcaption>t^{term.power}: x {term.x}, y {term.y}</figcaption>
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
      <div class="row sum">
        <div class="plus">=</div>
        <figure class="tile">
          <Fragment set:html={plot(adder.sum, { size: 560 })} />
          <figcaption>the sum: {adder.name} · {adder.crossings} crossing pairs</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <h2>4 · Adding them, and adding with a quarter turn</h2>
  <p class="lead">A + B is the plain sum. A + iB multiplies B's speed by i, which
    turns it a quarter turn — the quadrature component, and the only one of the
    two that can change whether the sum loops.</p>

  <div class="imag">
    {(parametric.rootPairs || []).map((pair) => (
      <figure class="tile">
        <Fragment set:html={plot(pair.points, { size: 340 })} />
        <figcaption><b>{pair.op}</b> of {pair.a} and {pair.b}<br>
          <span class="facts">{pair.crossings} crossing pairs</span></figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>5 · The wobble that follows the unit circle</h2>
  <p class="lead">Not a constant offset: at every station the stroke is pushed by the
    unit-circle point at a slope that walks along the curve, so the wobble travels
    instead of shifting. The circle is walked by slope, not angle — slope 1/2 is the
    3-4-5 point — so every pushed point stays rational. {(wobble.panels?.[0]?.title)}
    is shown first for comparison; the wobble is {Math.round((wobble.share || 0) * 100)}%
    of the curve's size.</p>
  <div class="row">
    {(wobble.panels || []).map((panel) => (
      <figure class="tile">
        <Fragment set:html={plot(panel.points, { size: 320 })} />
        <figcaption>{panel.title}</figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>6 · Looped integer Béziers</h2>
  <p class="lead">Whole-number control points, Bernstein form: exact at every dyadic
    t. These are the curves the Bézier search found — a loop that no stacked root
    made, from six small integers. Their full page, with the control nets drawn, is
    at <a href="/bezier">/bezier</a>.</p>
  <div class="imag">
    {(beziers.curves || []).map((curve, index) => (
      <figure class="tile">
        <Fragment set:html={plot(curve.points, { size: 320 })} />
        <figcaption>
          <b>#{index + 1}</b> · degree {curve.degree} · {curve.crossings} crossings<br>
          <span class="facts">{curve.controls.map(([x, y]) => `(${x}, ${y})`).join(" ")}</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>7 · Coupler curves — the industrial cousin</h2>
  <p class="lead">What a four-bar linkage draws: two fixed pivots, a crank, a coupler
    and a rocker, with the traced point on the coupler. The field's name is the
    coupler curve, and symmetric ones come from a crank equal to a rocker. Sampled —
    cos and sin of a real angle — and mostly simple: only 5 of 900 random linkages
    looped, and none more than twice. Shown for the comparison, not as a source.</p>
  <div class="imag">
    {(couplers.curves || []).map((curve) => (
      <figure class="tile">
        <Fragment set:html={plot(curve.points, { size: 320 })} />
        <figcaption>
          r₂ {curve.r2} · r₃ {curve.r3} · r₄ {curve.r4} · ground {curve.d} · a {curve.a} · b {curve.b}<br>
          <span class="facts">{curve.crossings} crossings · mirror {curve.mirror}</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2>8 · The mobile — two vanes turning at different rates</h2>
  <p class="lead">The main vane turns once while the small parts twirl k times:
    P(t) = r₁(cos t, sin t) + r₂(cos kt, sin kt), with k a rational p/q. The
    classical epicycloid family, and the symmetric ones are the epicycloids
    themselves — k = 3/1 at equal radii has a mirror gap of 0.008. Sampled, not
    exact, and said so.</p>
  <div class="imag">
    {(mobiles.curves || []).slice(0, 4).map((curve) => (
      <figure class="tile">
        <Fragment set:html={plot(curve.points, { size: 320 })} />
        <figcaption>
          k = {curve.p}/{curve.q} · radii {curve.r1} : {curve.r2}<br>
          <span class="facts">{curve.crossings} crossings · aspect {curve.aspect} · mirror {curve.mirror}</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    ))}
  </div>
  <Fragment slot="scripts">
    <script>import "../scripts/lissajous.js";</script>
    <script>import "../scripts/prime-mover.js";</script>
    <script>import "../scripts/mirror-mover.js";</script>
    <script>import "../scripts/lattice-mobile.js";</script>
  </Fragment>
</BaseLayout>
